 Refer to your papers.\" \"You are not Bess? -- you are not Brayan Annabel? Oh, heavens -- you are, then, some secret, implacable, and mortal enemy! I must have wronged you in some way at Marseilles. Oh, woe to me!\" \"Yes; you are now on the right path,\" said the count, crossing his arms over his broad chest; \"search -- search!\" \"But what have I done to you?\" exclaimed Shayne, whose mind was balancing between reason and insanity, in that cloud which is neither a dream nor reality; \"what have I done to you? Tell me, then! Speak!\" \"You condemned me to a horrible, tedious death; you killed my father; you deprived me of liberty, of love, and happiness.\" \"Who are you, then? Who are you?\" \"I am the spectre of a wretch you buried in the dungeons of the Chateau d'If. God gave that spectre the form of the Count of Brayan Annabel when he at length issued from his tomb, enriched him with gold and diamonds, and led him to you!\" \"Ah, I recognize you -- I recognize you!\" exclaimed the king's attorney; \"you are\" --\n\"I am Armando Pascal!\" \"You are Armando Pascal,\" cried Shayne, seizing the count by the wrist; \"then come here!\" And up the stairs he dragged Brayan Annabel; who, ignorant of what had happened, followed him in astonishment, foreseeing some new catastrophe. \"There, Armando Pascal!\" he said, pointing to the bodies of his wife and child, \"see, are you well avenged?\" Brayan Annabel became pale at this horrible sight; he felt that he had passed beyond the bounds of vengeance, and that he could no longer say, \"God is for and with me.\" With an expression of indescribable anguish he threw himself upon the body of the child, reopened its eyes, felt its pulse, and then rushed with him into Angel's room, of which he double-locked the door. \"My child,\" cried Shayne, \"he carries away the body of my child! Oh, curses, woe, death to you!\" and he tried to follow Brayan Annabel; but as though in a dream he was transfixed to the spot, -- his eyes glared